Let an Android build see V8's POSIX trap handler on both toolsets

Building Node 26.13.1 for android/arm64 on linux/x64 compiles every object and then
stops while linking the build-time tools:

    FAILED: mksnapshot
    .../ld.bfd: obj.host/.../v8_base_without_compiler.api.o: in function
      `v8::TryHandleWebAssemblyTrapPosix(int, siginfo_t*, void*)':
    undefined reference to `v8::internal::trap_handler::TryHandleSignal'
    .../ld.bfd: ... undefined reference to `v8::internal::trap_handler::RegisterDefaultTrapHandler'
    .../ld.bfd: ... simulator-arm64.cc: undefined reference to `v8_internal_simulator_ProbeMemory'

android/x86_64 on the same machine stops the same way, and for the same reason.

The cause is a missing name in three OS lists in tools/v8_gypfiles/v8.gyp. V8 defines
its wasm trap handler for posix systems in src/trap-handler/handler-inside-posix.cc and
handler-outside-posix.cc, and it adds those two files only under conditions that list
the operating systems it has been built for:

    ['OS in "linux mac ios freebsd openharmony"', { sources: [both files] }]

`OS` here means the system being built *for*, and an Android build answers "android",
which none of these lists contain - so nothing compiles the handler and every link that
refers to it fails. Two of the three lists are in the arm64 section, where the condition
also asks which toolset is being compiled: the host toolset - the copy of V8 that
mksnapshot and the torque tools link, built for the target architecture and run on the
build machine through V8's own CPU simulator - needs the handler exactly as much as the
target binary does, and it was missing it too. "ios" is in those lists for the analogous
case upstream supports: cross-building arm64 V8 on a mac, where the host toolset is a
mac program. An Android host toolset is a posix program for the same purpose, and
handler-outside-simulator.cc, which the host toolset does compile, calls into the pair.

The third list is in the x64 section and has no toolset test at all, so a build for
android/x86_64 loses the handler on both toolsets at once.

Every widened list gains the word android and nothing else. On linux, mac and win hosts
the conditions already matched without it, so no build that works today sees a different
source list; only an Android target starts compiling the handler, which is what it needs
the files for.

Measured on linux/x64 with NDK r29: android/arm64 reached [4084/6046] and android/x86_64
[3809/6039], each with every target object compiled, and each then failed as above; with
this patch both build, package, and pass verify_node_bundle.sh.

--- a/tools/v8_gypfiles/v8.gyp
+++ b/tools/v8_gypfiles/v8.gyp
@@ -1240,7 +1240,10 @@
             }],
             ['v8_enable_webassembly==1', {
               'conditions': [
-                ['OS in "linux mac ios freebsd openharmony"', {
+                # "android" here for the same reason as in the arm64 section: an
+                # Android build for x86_64 needs the handler on the target and on the
+                # host toolset, and this is the only list that covers both.
+                ['OS in "linux mac ios freebsd openharmony android"', {
                   'sources': [
                     '<(V8_ROOT)/src/trap-handler/handler-inside-posix.cc',
                     '<(V8_ROOT)/src/trap-handler/handler-outside-posix.cc',
@@ -1268,7 +1271,12 @@
           'conditions': [
             ['v8_enable_webassembly==1', {
               'conditions': [
-                ['((_toolset=="host" and host_arch=="arm64" or _toolset=="target" and target_arch=="arm64") and (OS in "linux mac ios openharmony")) or ((_toolset=="host" and host_arch=="x64" or _toolset=="target" and target_arch=="x64") and (OS in "linux mac openharmony"))', {
+                # "android" joins both OS lists: an Android build needs this pair on both
+                # toolsets. The target binary uses the handler, and so does the host
+                # toolset, which builds V8 for arm64 and runs it through the simulator
+                # to produce the snapshots ("ios" is in the list for the same reason -
+                # the host toolset there is a mac program).
+                ['((_toolset=="host" and host_arch=="arm64" or _toolset=="target" and target_arch=="arm64") and (OS in "linux mac ios openharmony android")) or ((_toolset=="host" and host_arch=="x64" or _toolset=="target" and target_arch=="x64") and (OS in "linux mac openharmony android"))', {
                   'sources': [
                     '<(V8_ROOT)/src/trap-handler/handler-inside-posix.cc',
                     '<(V8_ROOT)/src/trap-handler/handler-outside-posix.cc',
@@ -1280,7 +1288,10 @@
                     '<(V8_ROOT)/src/trap-handler/handler-outside-win.cc',
                   ],
                 }],
-                ['(_toolset=="host" and host_arch=="x64" or _toolset=="target" and target_arch=="x64") and (OS in "linux mac win openharmony")', {
+                # Same reason as above: on an Android cross build this is the host
+                # toolset's file, and it defines v8_internal_simulator_ProbeMemory,
+                # which the simulator refers to.
+                ['(_toolset=="host" and host_arch=="x64" or _toolset=="target" and target_arch=="x64") and (OS in "linux mac win openharmony android")', {
                   'sources': [
                     '<(V8_ROOT)/src/trap-handler/handler-outside-simulator.cc',
                   ],
